>
    <Section level={3} title="Plot won revenue against target by month">
        <p><code>chart</code> takes the series over one pair of axes and hands back parts to
            spread on markup of your own. A pointer over the <code>svg</code> moves a crosshair to
            the nearest month and opens every series' value there in the <code>tip</code>.</p>
        <Example code={wonAgainstTarget}><WonAgainstTarget /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Read the chart from the keyboard">
        <p>The <code>svg</code> takes focus. The arrows step its crosshair one x at a time and stop
            at the ends, Home and End go to the first and the last, Enter picks the x it stands at,
            and Escape puts it away. The readout describes the chart while it shows, and each step
            is read out through <code>announce</code>.</p>
        <Example code={keyboardReadout}><KeyboardReadout /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Fill the box a layout gives the chart">
        <p>Size the <code>svg</code> with classes. It measures itself and redraws at the new size
            whenever its box changes, so its labels are never stretched.</p>
        <Example code={fillTheBox}><FillTheBox /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Mark every point of a line with a dot of your own">
        <p><code>chart</code> hands back each mark as data with a part to spread, so the markup is
            yours: here a circle at every value <code>points</code> answers, over the lines.</p>
        <Example code={pointsOverLines}><PointsOverLines /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Write each column's value above the column">
        <p>A bar carries where it is drawn, so a label of your own stands at its top.</p>
        <Example code={barLabels}><BarLabels /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Shade a target band behind the line">
        <p><code>y</code> maps a value onto the plot and <code>frame</code> answers its edges, so a
            drawing of your own lines up with the marks the parts draw.</p>
        <Example code={targetBand}><TargetBand /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Draw won revenue as columns under a target line">
        <p>Each series' <code>kind</code> is how it is drawn: a line, a column per value or an
            area. <code>marks</code> answers them all on one pair of axes, in the order given, the
            first at the back.</p>
        <Example code={columnsUnderLine}><ColumnsUnderLine /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Stand two columns side by side in each month">
        <p>Columns in no stack share their month's slot, each up from zero.</p>
        <Example code={sideBySide}><SideBySide /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Stack won and lost deals in one column per month">
        <p><code>stack</code> piles every series that names no stack of its own into one stack,
            each month's values in the order the series are given.</p>
        <Example code={stackWonLost}><StackWonLost /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Read each month's wins as a share of the deals closed">
        <p><code>normalize</code> draws each stack as shares of its total, so every month
            reaches the top. The axis reads percentages, and the readout reads each count with
            its share.</p>
        <Example code={winShares}><WinShares /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Stack the pipeline's stages as areas">
        <p>Areas sharing a stack fill from the one given before them. A missing value counts as
            zero toward the stack, so the area above it carries on.</p>
        <Example code={stackedAreas}><StackedAreas /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Curve the stages through each month">
        <p>A series' <code>curve</code> draws its line or area as a curve through its values. It
            never swings past the two values it joins, so a stage never reads higher than its month
            did, and a stacked area follows the curve of the one under it.</p>
        <Example code={curvedAreas}><CurvedAreas /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Keep a target line out of a stacked chart">
        <p>A series' <code>stack: false</code> draws it at its own values when the chart stacks
            every other.</p>
        <Example code={targetOutOfStack}><TargetOutOfStack /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Group stacked columns, one group per team">
        <p>Series naming the same <code>stack</code> pile together, and each stack takes a band
            of its month's slot beside the others.</p>
        <Example code={groupedStacks}><GroupedStacks /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Hide a series with a checkbox">
        <p>Given as a function, <code>series</code> is read live: a series left out of the list
            leaves the chart, and the rest redraw without it.</p>
        <Example code={hideSeries}><HideSeries /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Compare two charts on one fixed axis">
        <p><code>bounds</code> fixes an axis's range rather than fitting it to the values, so two
            teams' charts read on the same scale.</p>
        <Example code={fixedAxis}><FixedAxis /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Ease columns between stacked and side by side">
        <p>A column is drawn by a CSS <code>transform</code> on the same element whatever its
            values become, so a transition on it animates every change.</p>
        <Example code={easeColumns}><EaseColumns /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Style the target as a dashed line">
        <p>Every mark carries <code>data-series</code>, the index of its series in the list, and
            draws in <code>currentColor</code>.</p>
        <Example code={dashedTarget}><DashedTarget /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Pick a month from the chart">
        <p><code>onpick</code> is called with the month nearest a click. A month
            <code>disabled</code> holds for takes no pick, and its columns and readout carry
            <code>data-disabled</code>.</p>
        <Example code={pickMonth}><PickMonth /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Draw won revenue by month, with a gap for a month not closed">
        <p><code>chart.scale</code> maps a value onto the drawing, and <code>chart.path</code>
            breaks the line at a <code>null</code> rather than drawing it down to zero.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { chart } from '@abide/abide'

            const won = [12_000, 18_000, null, 24_000, 31_000]
            const toX = chart.scale([0, won.length - 1], [0, 200])
            const toY = chart.scale([0, 40_000], [80, 0])
            const xs = won.map((_, index) => toX(index))
            const ys = won.map((value) => (value === null ? null : toY(value)))
            </script>

            <svg viewBox="0 0 200 80" role="img" aria-label="Won revenue">
                <path d={chart.path(xs, ys)} fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            </svg>
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Put gridlines on round values">
        <p><code>chart.ticks</code> answers values a step of 1, 2 or 5 times a power of ten apart,
            the first and last enclosing the range, so they double as the axis's ends.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`
            import { chart } from '@abide/abide'

            chart.ticks(3, 97, 5) // [0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100]
            chart.ticks(0, 1000, 2) // [0, 500, 1000]
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Fill the area under a sparkline">
        <p>Given a <code>baseline</code>, <code>chart.path</code> closes each run down to it, as a
            shape to fill.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`
            import { chart } from '@abide/abide'

            chart.path([0, 10], [1, 2], { baseline: 50 }) // 'M0,1L10,2L10,50L0,50Z'
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Pile won and lost deals into one stack">
        <p><code>chart.stack</code> answers where each series stands at each x: <code>low</code>
            is the top of what is under it and <code>high</code> its own top. Negative values pile
            down from zero.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`
            import { chart } from '@abide/abide'

            const [won, lost] = chart.stack(
                [
                    { label: 'Won', values: [4, 6] },
                    { label: 'Lost', values: [3, 2] },
                ],
                { stack: true },
            )
            lost.low // [4, 6]
            lost.high // [7, 8]
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section level={3} title="Find the month under the pointer">
        <p><code>chart.nearest</code> answers the index of the position nearest <code>x</code>,
            the earlier of two equally near, and <code>-1</code> where there are none.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="browser" code={`
            import { chart, reactive } from '@abide/abide'

            const xs = [0, 50, 100, 150, 200]
            const hovered = reactive(-1)
            const svg = document.querySelector('svg') as SVGSVGElement

            svg.addEventListener('pointermove', (event) => {
                const x = event.clientX - svg.getBoundingClientRect().left
                hovered.set(chart.nearest(xs, x))
            })
        `} />
    </Section>
</Doc>
